 Pruning followed, in three stages, each approved before execution, and together the stages removed 436 records: 382 papers, close to four hundred, plus 54 orphaned review, model, and pathway records that lost their anchors to the paper cuts. First, 95 records left as off-topic or orphaned: 88 papers removed as satellite topics (machine-learning and aerodynamics methods, flow sensing without neurophysiology, and fish swimming biomechanics), as duplicates, or as isolated records stranded by an earlier strict flagging pass, plus seven orphaned pathway, model, and review records. Second, a cut list of 290 papers assembled from explicit rules for duplicates, monographs, and conference abstracts, and from a weighted rubric ranking the remainder by citation count, age, rating, network isolation, and record thinness, while 351 protected records (foundational authors, feedback-linked papers, highly cited work, and pre-1940 classics) were excluded from consideration. Third, a final round removed four papers and 47 review records whose links had been emptied by the earlier cuts. The prune was deliberate on two grounds. The working base had swollen past the laboratory's free-plan 1,000-record cap, and a curated resource that answers a design question in ten records beats a haystack of a thousand; the audit had shown the corpus contained both, and the satellite material was answering nothing. No record was destroyed: every deletion was snapshotted, and the packaged explorer described below carries the 382 cut papers under an archived flag, so the audited corpus remains reconstructible in full.
 
-The audit and the prune are also why the laboratory built its own explorer app rather than adopting an existing tool. Airtable remains the working home of the data, but it is a curation tool, not a reader: it cannot travel past the laboratory's account, and a flat grid answers none of the questions this corpus exists to answer. An off-the-shelf bibliometric viewer was tried first and retired within weeks; it drew one crowded picture of the whole corpus, offered no drill-through from a cluster to the papers inside it, and let a handful of mega-cited classics dominate every view. The query patterns this resource must serve are specific: filter by afferent class, animal preparation, or pathway; follow a pathway record to the papers that established it in each species; walk the citation neighborhood of a candidate anchor paper; count the corpus by any two dimensions at once. A circuit designer wiring a reflex, an animal researcher choosing a preparation, and a roboticist choosing a sensor analog ask different questions of the same records, and no existing tool answered any of them. So the campaign packaged the corpus itself as a laboratory tool: a single-file, offline explorer embedding the audited corpus, the citation graph, and the named clusters, with searchable and pivotable tables, focus-mode citation neighborhoods drawn in the laboratory's own convention of papers as neurons with excitatory and inhibitory synapses, and a generated knowledge base with an index page plus cluster, pathway, afferent, and animal pages, all distributed as a zip that runs in a browser on any machine with no network connection.
